encyclopedia :still_mornings do
  name "Still Mornings"
  summary "Still mornings are windless resonance calms that usually form near dawn after a deep tide ebb under settled weather. For a few hours the bands lie flat, sound carries across whole regions, and trades that depend on hearing schedule their finest work into the window."
  kind :phenomenon
  subkind :physical_phenomenon
  status :complete
  topics :resonance, :navigation, :ecology, :music
  prevalence :common
  available_globally
  medium "The local resonance bands and the air itself, region-wide, for a span of hours"
  nature "A morning calm in which band activity drops to seasonal minimum and sound carries far past its ordinary range; loosely forecastable from the tide tables and the night's weather"
  hazard "Sound travels both ways — the calm that lets a listener hear a distant fault lets a distant listener hear everything else"

  descriptive_identity(
    signs: "The morning arrives flat and clear in every register at once: gauges resting, " \
           "gleeds dim, and the first struck bell audible three valleys over, arriving " \
           "small and perfect like something remembered.",
    effects: "Listening work saves itself for the window — span rounds, tasting walks, " \
             "singers' proofs, the long-distance hails between settlements that still " \
             "mornings make briefly possible — and musicians simply get up early.",
    hazards: "Work crews defer loud machinery until the calm lifts, while settlements " \
             "treat arguments and private business carried from afar as unheard."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    Some mornings the world goes flat and clear. A still morning most often follows a deep tide ebb under settled weather. Almanac offices forecast the window from tide tables and the night's weather, with less confidence than they give an ordinary weather notice. For a span of hours the bands rest at their seasonal floor while the air carries sound to extraordinary range: a struck bell audible three valleys over, a dock's morning horn arriving in a settlement that has never seen the dock, conversation from a boat carrying across a full reach of water with every word intact. The calm arrives at dawn across a whole region and burns off with the day's first real traffic and heat. Regional names for it commonly translate as the listening hours.

    The trades built their finest work into the window. Span listeners walk their most doubtful stations on still mornings, when the beams speak into silence; the singers prove their choir-true splices; #{encyclopedia_ref :palate, "glass tasters"} work through their most exacting lots; and the settlements that live within a calm's reach of each other keep the old custom of the morning hail. Each settlement's caller sends the town's name, its news in a sentence, and its regards, and the answering calls walk back across the distance in the order the sound arrives. The hail serves as navigation, neighborliness, and census, and a town whose call goes unanswered on a clear still morning sends someone to check by road.
  PROSE

  cue "The struck bell arrives from three valleys away, small and perfect, and everyone in the yard pauses in the same breath and says some version of 'still morning' before changing their plans."
  cue "The town's caller stands on the water tower at dawn sending the name, the news, and the regards, and the neighboring towns' answers walk back across the distance in arrival order."
  affordance "The calm is a free instrument upgrade for every listening trade — beams, splices, and tastings read at their truest — and the morning hail custom gives neighboring settlements a working census that costs one caller and one sentence."
  pressure "Listening crews claim towers and quiet yards before dawn, districts defer loud machinery until the calm lifts, and anything the air carries by accident is treated as unheard."
  variation "Valley country hails town to town in long chains. Ring settlements catch the calm less often, and their callers use a shorter form when voices carry between neighboring hulls."
  variation "Musicians treat the windows as the year's true performance slots, and the great still-morning concerts — a single player, audible across a whole region — are announced conditionally and remembered by date."
end
